nix-serve is a small server for sharing your Nix store over the network.

In your case:

Your NixOS PC IP = 172.16.17.171
nix-serve port  = 5000

So nix-serve makes this URL available:

http://172.16.17.171:5000

Your friend’s NixOS PC can use this URL as a local Nix binary cache.

Simple meaning

nix-serve does this:

Your /nix/store packages
        ↓
nix-serve
        ↓
Available on LAN using HTTP
        ↓
Friend's NixOS PC can download from your machine

Local Nix Cache Setup in NixOS

In NixOS, /nix/store contains installed packages.

Example:

/nix/store/abc123-firefox
/nix/store/xyz456-docker
/nix/store/pqr789-nmap

Now imagine you have 3 NixOS machines in your home/company:

Laptop
PC
Server

When you update all 3 machines, normally each machine downloads packages from the internet:

Laptop  → downloads Firefox from internet
PC      → downloads Firefox from internet again
Server  → downloads Firefox from internet again

This wastes internet and time.

What is Local Nix Cache?

Local Nix cache means you make one machine in your local network act like a package storage server.

Example:

NAS / Server = Local Nix Cache

Then the flow becomes:

Laptop downloads package once
        ↓
Package is saved in local cache
        ↓
PC and Server download from local cache instead of internet

So local Nix cache means:

A local network server that stores Nix packages,
so other NixOS machines can download from LAN instead of internet.

Architecture:

              cache.nixos.org
                    |
                    v
          Office Cache Server
             172.16.17.171
                    |
      --------------------------------
      |              |               |
   NixOS PC-1     NixOS PC-2      NixOS PC-3
Server Setup
Step 1: Generate Cache Keys

Run on your server NixOS machine:

sudo mkdir -p /var/cache-keys
cd /var/cache-keys

sudo nix-store --generate-binary-cache-key cache.local-1 cache-private-key.pem cache-public-key.pem

sudo chmod 600 cache-private-key.pem
sudo chmod 644 cache-public-key.pem

Now show your public key:

cat /var/cache-keys/cache-public-key.pem

You will get output like this:

cache.local-1:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx=

Copy this public key. You will need it on the client PC.

Step 2: Create Separate nix-serve.nix File

On the server PC, run:

sudo nano /etc/nixos/nix-serve.nix

Paste this:

{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
    nix-serve
  ];

  services.nix-serve = {
    enable = true;
    port = 5000;
    secretKeyFile = "/var/cache-keys/cache-private-key.pem";
  };

  networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts = [ 5000 ];
}

Save the file:

Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X
Step 3: Import nix-serve.nix in Main configuration.nix

Open the main configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/nixos/configuration.nix

Find the imports section and add ./nix-serve.nix.

Example:

imports =
  [
    ./hardware-configuration.nix
    ./nix-serve.nix
  ];

Now rebuild the server:

sudo nixos-rebuild switch

Check the nix-serve service status:

systemctl status nix-serve

It should show:

active (running)
Step 4: Test Server Cache Locally

Run this on the server PC:

curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/nix-cache-info

Expected output:

StoreDir: /nix/store
WantMassQuery: 1
Priority: 30

Now test using the server IP:

curl http://172.16.17.171:5000/nix-cache-info

Expected output:

StoreDir: /nix/store
WantMassQuery: 1
Priority: 30

If both commands work, your server cache is running correctly.

Client Setup
Step 5: Create Separate Client Cache File

On the client PC, run:

sudo nano /etc/nixos/client.nix

Paste this:

{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  nix.settings = {
    substituters = [
      "http://172.16.17.171:5000"
      "https://cache.nixos.org/"
    ];

    trusted-public-keys = [
      "cache.local-1:PASTE-YOUR-PUBLIC-KEY-HERE"
      "cache.nixos.org-1:6NCHdD59X431o0gWypbMrAURkbJ16ZPMQFGspcDShjY="
    ];
  };
}

Now replace this:

cache.local-1:PASTE-YOUR-PUBLIC-KEY-HERE

with your real public key from the server.

Example:

"cache.local-1:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx="

Final example:

{ config, pkgs, ... }:

{
  nix.settings = {
    substituters = [
      "http://172.16.17.171:5000"
      "https://cache.nixos.org/"
    ];

    trusted-public-keys = [
      "cache.local-1:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx="
      "cache.nixos.org-1:6NCHdD59X431o0gWypbMrAURkbJ16ZPMQFGspcDShjY="
    ];
  };
}

Save the file:

Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X
Step 6: Import Client File in Client configuration.nix

On the client PC, open:

sudo nano /etc/nixos/configuration.nix

Change the imports section to:

imports =
  [
    ./hardware-configuration.nix
    ./client.nix
  ];
Step 7: Rebuild Client

Run on the client PC:

sudo nixos-rebuild switch

Restart the Nix daemon:

sudo systemctl restart nix-daemon
Step 8: Check Client Config Applied or Not

Run this on the client PC:

nix show-config | grep -E "substituters|trusted-public-keys"

You should see something like:

substituters = http://172.16.17.171:5000 https://cache.nixos.org/
trusted-public-keys = cache.local-1:... cache.nixos.org-1:...

If you see your local cache URL, then the client setup is correct.

Testing with Package Install
Step 9: Install Package on Server

On the server PC, run:

nix-shell -p cowsay

Then exit:

exit

This will make sure cowsay exists in the server’s /nix/store.

Step 10: Install Package on Client

Because you already added the local cache permanently in client.nix, you do not need to run the big command every time.

On the client PC, simply run:

nix-shell -p cowsay

Then test:

cowsay hello

Expected output:

 _______
< hello >
 -------
        \   ^__^
         \  (oo)\_______
            (__)\       )\/\
                ||----w |
                ||     ||
Important Note

This big command is only for temporary testing:

nix-shell -p cowsay --option substituters "http://172.16.17.171:5000 https://cache.nixos.org/"

After adding the cache permanently in client.nix, you only need:

nix-shell -p cowsay

Nix will automatically check your local cache first:

http://172.16.17.171:5000

Then it will check the official NixOS cache:

https://cache.nixos.org/
